Similar peaks were observed for the T6-treated composites in Fig.  7 b. The 0T6 pattern shows the baseline intensities for all peaks. No separate peaks are observed for the Mg 2 Si precipitates formed during T6 [ 54 ]. This is because they have very fine particles and their reflections overlap with strong Al peaks. With DPSA addition and T6 treatment, the Al (200) and Al (220) peaks became progressively more intense particularly at 0.75T6 and 1.0T6. The Si peaks also sharpened slightly, reflecting a more refined and stable eutectic Si dispersion. These changes in intensity indicate mild texture development, reduced micro-strain and improved lattice coherence. The presence of thermally stable oxide phases such as Fe 2 O 3 , SiO 2 , and MgO within the DPSA contributed to the improved thermomechanical and thermal behaviour of the composites. The absence of distinct DPSA-derived peaks in the composite spectra is attributed to the low reinforcement loading (< 1 wt%) and the dominance of the  \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
